## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is where all content state originates and is distributed to every consumer via useContent (236 callers). Currently content is held as a static memoized value from defaultContent — it never updates after mount. For Live Preview to work, this provider must subscribe to Contentstack live preview events and reactively update the content state, which will automatically propagate to all useContent consumers without any per-component change.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the `useMemo` block (lines 56-61) with `useState` initialized from `defaultContent`, and add a `useEffect` that calls the Contentstack Stack's `onEntryChange` callback (from the already-installed `contentstack` package) to merge incoming preview data into state via the setter. This makes content reactive: when the CMS pushes a preview update, the setter fires, `content` changes, `useContent` re-renders all 236 consumers automatically. The `useContent` hook itself needs zero changes — it already depends on `content` via `useContentstackContext()`. Pattern: `const [content, setContent] = useState(defaultContent); useEffect(() => { Stack.onEntryChange((data) => { setContent(prev => deepMerge(prev, data)); }); }, []);` — use the `contentstack` SDK's built-in `onEntryChange` (no new package needed; `contentstack` is already in node_modules).


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a content author opens a page in the Contentstack CMS preview, the previewed page displays the current published content for that page.
- When a content author edits a text field in the CMS while the preview is open, the corresponding text on the previewed page updates to show the new value without the author manually refreshing the page.
- When a content author replaces or edits an image in the CMS while the preview is open, the corresponding image on the previewed page updates to show the new image without a manual page refresh.
- When a content author edits structured or tabular content in the CMS while the preview is open, the corresponding content on the previewed page updates to reflect the edits without a manual page refresh.
- When a content author edits several distinct pieces of content shown on the same previewed page, each edited piece updates on the previewed page, demonstrating the live update applies across the whole page rather than to a single spot.
- After a content author saves an edit in the CMS, the change becomes visible on the previewed page promptly, within a few seconds, without any manual action by the author.
- When the same page is viewed outside the CMS preview context, it loads and displays its content normally with no errors or blank regions.

